\details{
This is one of the most classical string art constructions. Pegs are placed
on two perpendicular axes. In the basic case, the peg at position \code{i} on the
horizontal axis is connected to the peg at position \code{i} on the vertical axis,
where the vertical axis is indexed from top to bottom.

For \code{k = 1}, the construction corresponds to the standard family of segments
joining points \verb{(t, 0)} and \verb{(0, 1 - t)}, after scaling by \code{width} and
\code{height}. Its ideal envelope satisfies
\code{sqrt(x / width) + sqrt(y / height) = 1}.

For \code{k > 1}, the function adds shifted sweeps of the same construction,
producing denser string art patterns while preserving the same pedagogical
idea of a family of straight lines generating a curved envelope.
}
